      <h3 style="margin: 0 0 0.5rem 0; color: var(--accent-blue);">💡 Quick Summary for Evaluator</h3>
      <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.95rem;">
        LaunchForge is an <strong>MVC-based Full-Stack Web Application</strong>. It uses <strong>Node.js &amp; Express</strong> for the backend API &amp; route orchestration, <strong>EJS (Embedded JavaScript)</strong> for Server-Side Rendering (SSR) of dynamic UI views, and <strong>MongoDB Atlas</strong> via Mongoose ODM for cloud document storage.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>1. Mandatory Tech Stack &amp; Architecture</h2>
    <p style="color: var(--text-muted); line-height: 1.6;">
      • <strong>Backend:</strong> Node.js (v18+) with Express.js micro-framework.<br>
      • <strong>Frontend SSR:</strong> EJS view engine with partial templates (header, navbar, footer).<br>
      • <strong>Database:</strong> MongoDB Atlas connected via Mongoose 8.x ODM.<br>
      • <strong>Authentication:</strong> Express sessions + <code>bcryptjs</code> password hashing.<br>
      • <strong>Design System:</strong> Pure Vanilla CSS with dark mode, Glassmorphism backdrop-filters, custom micro-interactions.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Project Directory Structure (MVC Pattern)</h2>
    <pre><code>launchforge/
├── config/
│   └── db.js                 <-- MongoDB Atlas connection handler
├── models/
│   ├── User.js               <-- Schema for User registration & password hashing
│   ├── Product.js            <-- Schema for Products, category enum & upvotes array
│   └── Comment.js            <-- Schema for Product discussion & feedback
├── controllers/
│   ├── authController.js     <-- Login, Signup, Session destruction, User Profile
│   └── productController.js  <-- Product CRUD, Upvote toggling, Search & Filters
├── middleware/
│   └── authMiddleware.js     <-- Protected route guard & template locals injection
├── routes/
│   ├── authRoutes.js         <-- Routes under /auth/ (login, register, logout)
│   ├── productRoutes.js      <-- Routes under /products/ (submit, detail, upvote)
│   └── indexRoutes.js        <-- Homepage, Leaderboard, Docs routes
├── views/
│   ├── partials/             <-- Reusable EJS headers, navbar, footers
│   ├── index.ejs             <-- Home product listing page
│   ├── product-detail.ejs    <-- Single product detailed view + comments
│   └── submit-product.ejs    <-- Product submission form
└── server.js                 <-- Express server entry point</code></pre>

    <h2>3. Frequently Asked Viva Questions &amp; Answers</h2>

    <h3>Q1: What is Server-Side Rendering (SSR) with EJS and how does it work here?</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--text-muted); line-height: 1.6;">
      <strong>Answer:</strong> In SSR, when a user requests a URL (e.g. <code>GET /</code>), Express handles the request on the server, fetches product documents from MongoDB Atlas via Mongoose, and executes <code>res.render('index', { products })</code>. EJS compiles HTML on the server by interpolating JavaScript variables into template tags (e.g., <code>&lt;%= product.title %&gt;</code>) before sending complete HTML straight to the browser.
    </p>

    <h3>Q2: How does LaunchForge connect to MongoDB Atlas?</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--text-muted); line-height: 1.6;">
      <strong>Answer:</strong> In <code>config/db.js</code>, we import Mongoose and invoke <code>mongoose.connect(process.env.MONGODB_URI)</code>. The <code>MONGODB_URI</code> contains our MongoDB Atlas connection string (e.g., <code>mongodb+srv://user:pass@cluster.mongodb.net/dbname</code>). Mongoose connects asynchronously and provides ODM features like schemas, validation, and reference populating.
    </p>

    <h3>Q3: How are passwords secured in the database?</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--text-muted); line-height: 1.6;">
      <strong>Answer:</strong> In <code>models/User.js</code>, we use a Mongoose <code>pre('save')</code> hook powered by <code>bcryptjs</code>. Before saving a user record, bcrypt generates a salt and hashes the plain text password. Plain passwords are never stored in MongoDB. During login, <code>user.comparePassword()</code> verifies the hash safely.
    </p>

    <h3>Q4: How does Upvoting work in the Product model?</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--text-muted); line-height: 1.6;">
      <strong>Answer:</strong> The <code>Product</code> model has an <code>upvotes</code> array field containing ObjectIds referencing <code>User</code> models: <code>upvotes: [{ type: Schema.Types.ObjectId, ref: 'User' }]</code>. When a user clicks Upvote, <code>postUpvoteProduct</code> checks if the user's ObjectId is in the array. If present, it removes it (toggle off); if absent, it pushes it (toggle on), and saves the document.
    </p>

    <h3>Q5: How is session state preserved across pages?</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--text-muted); line-height: 1.6;">
      <strong>Answer:</strong> We use <code>express-session</code> middleware in <code>server.js</code>. Upon successful login, we store <code>req.session.user = { id, username, email }</code>. Our custom middleware <code>setUserLocals</code> copies <code>req.session.user</code> to <code>res.locals.currentUser</code>, making user status instantly available in all EJS templates for conditional UI rendering.
    </p>
